A note here: if you're not already a programmer, the NetHack code is not
the very best place to start. It's hardly a schoolbook example of well-
planned, easily readable code; it has accreted through the years, and it
shows this.

> > I have Kernighan and Ritchie's book (2nd Edition "ANSI C", though
> > NetHack ain't ANSI C) and it describes the language quite well
> > (Ritchie is also the creator of C). I think it's quite a good C
> > tutorial, too, but am not 100% sure it will serve you well, if C is to
> > be your first programming language. I'd recommend checking it out in
> > any case though...
> 
> I gotta disagree here.  It's a good reference but pretty sucky as a
> tutorial.

Beg to differ. IMO it's the best tutorial for a programming language
I've ever seen. It is clear, it is concise, it is direct, and it has
plenty of exercises for you to cut your teeth on. True, you need to pay
attention, and if you think you can get away with trying to understand
the text without doing the exercises you're going to be disappointed,
but then, there is no royal road to programming.
Of course, another question is whether it is a good idea at all to learn
programming with C as your first language. IYAM, it isn't; C is a great
programming language, but it is so for the very same reasons which make
it not so good a teaching language. For similar reasons, something like
Pascal is great for learning to program in but a pain to actually use
for serious work.

>  I don't think it is even marketed as a tutorial.

Chapter one is called "A Tutorial Introduction". However: "This book is
not an introductory programming manual; it assumes some familiarity with
basic programming concepts..." It is a tutorial on C, not on beginning
to program. Of course, I think this is a good thing, given my views on C
as a learning language.

> I'd just go to the store and thumb through a few.

Just make sure you skip the "in 24 hours" and "for dummies" books; those
titles tend to describe the writing of the book at least as much as the
reading of it.

